Hybrid Tyre-to-PMEDS Load Transfer for Ground-Level Magnetic Roads: Velocity-Dependent Levitation, Gap-Constrained Control, and Fault-Tolerant Urban Mobility

Ouadi Maakoul · Claude · ChatGPT · DeepSeek

Revision 4

---

Abstract

This paper establishes the mathematical foundations for a ground-level magnetic road architecture in which ordinary road vehicles continuously exchange vertical load between pneumatic tyres and permanent-magnet electrodynamic suspension (PMEDS). Unlike attractive electromagnetic suspension (EMS), which requires an overhead ferromagnetic rail, PMEDS acts from a conductive road surface below the vehicle: relative motion between onboard Halbach arrays and embedded conductive corridors induces eddy currents that produce repulsive lift.

We derive the Inductrack lift and drag force laws from explicit Halbach and track parameters, correct the critical levitation velocity relationship, and introduce a gap-parameterized three-velocity hierarchy: v_{\rm disengage}(g)<v_{\rm phys}(g)<v_{\rm rob}(g)<v_{\rm engage}(g). We redefine the load fraction as a normalized support ratio \lambda=\sum F_M/(\sum F_M+\sum F_W)\in[0,1], and introduce a second variable \epsilon_F=F_M+F_W+F_{\rm active}-mg for the vertical force balance. We separate the passive PMEDS force from the bidirectional active correction F_{\rm active}(u), which is the sole control input with first-order actuator dynamics. We construct a hybrid feasible domain \mathcal{D}_H in the (v,g) plane that correctly accounts for the active force contribution. The safety envelope includes a transition rate constraint |\dot\lambda|\le\dot\lambda_{\max} derived from the true time derivative of \lambda. A lateral guidance architecture decision is made explicit: hybrid passive-perforated plus active. Fault tolerance is formulated as a capture-basin inclusion to the ordinary wheel-supported set. The vehicle dynamics are embedded in a hybrid graph G=(V,E_0\cup E_M). Technical specifications are provided for a 500 kg reference vehicle.

---

1. Introduction

1.1 From Overhead Rail to Ground-Level Magnetic Road

Attractive electromagnetic suspension (EMS) requires an overhead ferromagnetic rail and is therefore a guideway architecture. Ground-level magnetic support must push the vehicle upward from below, which requires repulsive electrodynamic suspension. The architecture is:

\text{vehicle} \downarrow \text{PM/Halbach array} \downarrow \text{conductive magnetic road}

The vehicle keeps pneumatic tyres for ordinary roads, standstill, low speed, and fault recovery.

1.2 The Central Research Question

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Can a normal road vehicle continuously exchange load between tyres and}\\
&\text{ground-based electrodynamic magnetic suspension,}\\
&\text{while remaining controllable, safe, fault-tolerant, and usable}\\
&\text{in stop-and-go urban traffic?}
\end{aligned}
}

1.3 Contributions

Seven contributions: (i) correct physical architecture; (ii) reduced-order explicit derivation of F_\infty(g) from Halbach/track parameters; (iii) corrected levitation velocity and gap-parameterized three-velocity hierarchy; (iv) redefined load fraction \lambda\in[0,1] and separate force-balance variable \epsilon_F; (v) passive/active separation with bidirectional actuator dynamics and true transition rate constraint; (vi) hybrid feasible domain \mathcal{D}_H with power budget and lateral guidance decision; (vii) capture-basin fault tolerance and hybrid corridor graph.

