2. Electrodynamic Force Model

2.1 Halbach Array Field

A Halbach array with wavelength \lambda, wavenumber k=2\pi/\lambda, and peak surface field B_0 produces:

B_y(x,y)=B_0\cos(kx)e^{-ky},\qquad B_x(x,y)=B_0\sin(kx)e^{-ky}

The peak field is related to the magnet remanent field B_r, magnet thickness d, and number M of blocks per wavelength:

B_0=B_r\left(1-e^{-kd}\right)\frac{\sin(\pi/M)}{\pi/M}

2.2 Reduced-Order Explicit Derivation of F_\infty(g)

For a track of close-packed shorted circuits with inductance L, resistance R, spacing d_c, and array width w, the high-speed limiting lift force per unit area of Halbach array is:

\frac{F_\infty}{A_H}=\frac{B_0^2 w}{2kLd_c}e^{-2kg}

This expression is the standard Inductrack result for the limiting levitation force per unit area, derived from the circuit equation V=L\dot I+RI=\omega\varphi_0\cos(\omega t) in the limit v\gg v_t. Multiplying by the array area A_H=wL_H, where L_H is the array length along motion:

\boxed{
F_\infty(g)=\frac{B_0^2 w^2 L_H}{2kLd_c}e^{-2kg}
}

Status. This is a reduced-order geometric model. It should be validated against FEM or 2D/3D numerical models before quantitative design commitment.

Dimensional check. [B_0^2]={\rm T}^2={\rm kg}^2/({\rm A}^2{\rm s}^4); [w^2L_H]={\rm m}^3; [k]={\rm m}^{-1}; [L]={\rm H}={\rm kg}\,{\rm m}^2/({\rm A}^2{\rm s}^2); [d_c]={\rm m}. Then:

\frac{{\rm kg}^2{\rm m}^3/({\rm A}^2{\rm s}^4)}{{\rm m}^{-1}\cdot{\rm kg}\,{\rm m}^2/({\rm A}^2{\rm s}^2)\cdot{\rm m}}
=\frac{{\rm kg}^2{\rm m}^3}{{\rm kg}\,{\rm m}^2}
={\rm kg}\,{\rm m}/{\rm s}^2={\rm N}

Dimensionally consistent.

Reference parameters. Using B_0=0.8 T, \lambda=0.2 m, k=31.4 rad/m, w=0.5 m, L_H=1.2 m, L=2\,\muH, d_c=0.05 m:

F_\infty(0)=\frac{(0.8)^2(0.5)^2(1.2)}{2(31.4)(2\times 10^{-6})(0.05)}=\frac{0.192}{6.28\times 10^{-6}}\approx 30573\,{\rm N}

At g=20 mm:

F_\infty(0.020)=30573\,e^{-2(31.4)(0.020)}=30573\,e^{-1.256}\approx 8713\,{\rm N}

2.3 Inductrack Lift Force

\boxed{
F_M(v,g)=\frac{F_\infty(g)}{1+(v_t/v)^2}
}

with transition velocity:

\boxed{
v_t=\frac{\lambda}{2\pi}\frac{R}{L}=\frac{R}{kL}
}

This is the speed at which the lift has risen to one-half of its asymptotic value and the lift-to-drag ratio is unity.

2.4 Magnetic Drag Force

By energy balance, F_D=P_{\rm loss}/v:

\boxed{
F_D(v,g)=F_M(v,g)\frac{v_t}{v}
}

Lift-to-drag ratio:

\frac{F_M}{F_D}=\frac{v}{v_t}

2.5 Power Dissipation

\boxed{
P_D(v,g)=F_D(v,g)v=F_\infty(g)\frac{v_t}{1+(v_t/v)^2}
=F_\infty(g)v_t\frac{v^2}{v^2+v_t^2}
}

Proposition 2.1. P_D(v) is monotonically increasing and saturates at P_D^\infty=F_\infty v_t.

Proof. Differentiate:

\frac{dP_D}{dv}=F_\infty v_t\frac{2vv_t^2}{(v^2+v_t^2)^2}>0

Limit: \lim_{v\to\infty}P_D=F_\infty v_t. ∎

2.6 Critical Levitation Velocity

Theorem 1 (Physical levitation velocity). Define v_{\rm phys}(g) by F_M(v_{\rm phys},g)=mg. Then:

\boxed{
v_{\rm phys}(g)=\frac{v_t}{\sqrt{F_\infty(g)/mg-1}}
}

provided F_\infty(g)>mg. If F_\infty(g)\le mg, full magnetic support is impossible at gap g.

Proof. From mg=F_\infty/[1+(v_t/v_{\rm phys})^2]:

1+(v_t/v_{\rm phys})^2=F_\infty/mg

(v_t/v_{\rm phys})^2=F_\infty/mg-1

v_{\rm phys}=v_t/\sqrt{F_\infty/mg-1}

Real and positive iff F_\infty>mg. ∎

2.7 Gap-Parameterized Three-Velocity Hierarchy

Definition 1. The three-velocity hierarchy is gap-parameterized:

Physical levitation: v_{\rm phys}(g):F_M(v,g)=mg.

Robust sustainment: v_{\rm rob}(g)=\inf\{v:F_M(v,g)\ge mg+F_{\rm margin}\}, with

F_{\rm margin}=F_{\rm dist}+F_{\rm ctrl}+F_{\rm model}+F_{\rm roll/pitch}

Engagement/disengagement:

\boxed{
v_{\rm disengage}(g)<v_{\rm phys}(g)<v_{\rm rob}(g)<v_{\rm engage}(g)
}

Constraint 1 (Constant-velocity hierarchy). If v_{\rm disengage} and v_{\rm engage} are constant, they must satisfy:

\boxed{
v_{\rm disengage}
<
\inf_{g\in[g_{\min},g_{\max}]}v_{\rm phys}(g)
}

\boxed{
v_{\rm engage}
>
\sup_{g\in[g_{\min},g_{\max}]}v_{\rm rob}(g)
}

For the reference vehicle with g\in[15,25] mm:

Gap F_\infty v_{\rm phys}
15 mm \approx 11.92 kN \approx 1.34 m/s
20 mm \approx 8.71 kN \approx 1.82 m/s
25 mm \approx 6.36 kN \approx 2.94 m/s

Thus v_{\rm disengage}=1.5 m/s satisfies 1.5<1.34 only at g=15 mm; for larger gaps, v_{\rm phys} exceeds 1.5 m/s. The constant hierarchy requires v_{\rm disengage}<\inf_g v_{\rm phys}(g)\approx 1.34 m/s.

2.8 Passive Vertical Stability and Sensitivity

Theorem 2 (Passive vertical stability). For F_\infty(g)=Ce^{-2kg} with C>0, the vertical stiffness is:

\frac{\partial F_M}{\partial g}=-\frac{2kCe^{-2kg}}{1+(v_t/v)^2}<0

The passive vertical equilibrium is stable in the absence of lateral dynamics.

Proof. Direct differentiation. Negative stiffness means downward perturbation increases lift (restoring). ∎

Theorem 3 (Lift sensitivity to gap and speed). For

F_M(v,g)=F_{\infty,0}e^{-2kg}\frac{v^2}{v^2+v_t^2},

the relative sensitivities are:

\boxed{
\frac{\partial\ln F_M}{\partial g}=-2k
}

\boxed{
\frac{\partial\ln F_M}{\partial v}
=
\frac{2v_t^2}{v(v^2+v_t^2)}
}

Proof. Direct differentiation. ∎

With k=31.4 m⁻¹, S_g=-62.8 m⁻¹. An increase in gap of 1 mm multiplies the force by approximately e^{-0.0628}\approx 0.939, i.e. a \approx 6.1\% decrease in lift per millimetre. The gap is not merely a safety variable; it is a major physical gain of the PMEDS system.

2.9 Lateral Stability and Guidance Architecture Decision

Proposition 2.3. A Halbach array over a uniform conductive sheet has zero lateral force at centered position and non-positive lateral stiffness. Vertical PMEDS lift alone does not guarantee lateral stability.

The lateral force decomposes as:

F_y^{\rm lateral}=F_y^{\rm passive}+F_y^{\rm geometry}+F_y^{\rm active}

Architecture decision. For a road vehicle, the realistic near-term choice is a hybrid architecture:

\boxed{
\text{Lateral guidance = passive perforated guideway + active correction coils}
}

· Passive layer. A perforated guideway with periodic symmetric rectangular apertures reshapes eddy currents into an electromagnetic potential well, producing lateral restoring force without auxiliary magnets or coils. Recent experimental work has investigated this mechanism and demonstrated self-centering guidance.
· Active layer. Control coils handle large perturbations, asymmetric loading, and transients that exceed the passive restoring authority. Controller bandwidths of several hundred rad/s (tens of Hz) are achievable; a design target of \ge 100 Hz is reasonable.

