Appendix A: Inductrack Force Law Derivation

A.1 Circuit Equation

The equivalent circuit of one track circuit consists of an inductance L and resistance R in series. The moving Halbach array induces a time-varying flux \phi(t)=\phi_0\sin(\omega t), where \omega=kv=2\pi v/\lambda. The circuit equation is:

\boxed{
V=L\frac{dI}{dt}+RI=\omega\phi_0\cos(\omega t)
}

Assumption. The conductor width d_c in the direction of motion is small compared to k^{-1}=\lambda/(2\pi), ensuring that the flux enclosed by each circuit is approximately constant over the circuit width.

A.2 Steady-State Solution

The steady-state solution of the circuit equation is:

I(t)=\frac{\phi_0}{L}\frac{1}{1+(R/\omega L)^2}
\left[\sin(\omega t)+\frac{R}{\omega L}\cos(\omega t)\right]

This can be written as:

I(t)=\frac{\phi_0}{\sqrt{R^2+(\omega L)^2}}\cos(\omega t-\phi),
\qquad
\tan\phi=\frac{\omega L}{R}

In the high-speed limit \omega\gg R/L, the phase shifts by 90^\circ so that the current is in phase with the flux, maximizing the lifting force:

I(t)\to\frac{\phi_0}{L}\sin(\omega t)

The transition speed is defined as the speed at which \omega L=R, i.e., \omega=R/L:

\boxed{
v_t=\frac{R}{kL}
}

At this speed, the lift-to-drag ratio is unity and the lift has reached one-half of its asymptotic value.

A.3 Time-Averaged Lift Force

The time-averaged lift force on one circuit is:

\langle F_y\rangle=\frac12\frac{k\phi_0^2}{L}
\frac{(\omega L/R)^2}{1+(\omega L/R)^2}

Substituting \omega=kv and v_t=R/(kL):

\boxed{
\langle F_y\rangle=\frac12\frac{k\phi_0^2}{L}
\frac{v^2}{v^2+v_t^2}
}

The peak flux enclosed by a circuit of width w and height extending over the Halbach array surface is:

\phi_0=\frac{B_0 w}{k}e^{-kg}

where g=y_t is the gap between the Halbach array surface and the upper conductor of the track circuit. Substituting:

\langle F_y\rangle=\frac{B_0^2 w^2}{2kL}
\frac{v^2}{v^2+v_t^2}e^{-2kg}

For N circuits per unit track length, with N=1/d_c, the force per unit area of Halbach array is:

\boxed{
\frac{\sum\langle F_y\rangle}{A_H}
=\frac{B_0^2 w}{2kLd_c}e^{-2kg}
\frac{v^2}{v^2+v_t^2}
}

A.4 Asymptotic Lift Force

In the high-speed limit v\gg v_t:

\boxed{
F_\infty(g)=\frac{B_0^2 w^2 L_H}{2kLd_c}e^{-2kg}
}

where A_H=wL_H is the Halbach array area. This is the reduced-order geometric model used in Section 2.2.

A.5 Lift and Drag Force Laws

Defining:

F_M(v,g)=\frac{F_\infty(g)}{1+(v_t/v)^2}

The drag force follows from energy balance. The power dissipated in the track circuits is P_{\rm loss}=\langle I^2R\rangle. The drag force is F_D=P_{\rm loss}/v. Since the lift-to-drag ratio is F_M/F_D=v/v_t:

\boxed{
F_D(v,g)=F_M(v,g)\frac{v_t}{v}
}

A.6 Power Dissipation

P_D(v,g)=F_D(v,g)v=F_M(v,g)v_t
=F_\infty(g)\frac{v_t v^2}{v^2+v_t^2}

This is monotonically increasing with the limit:

\boxed{
P_D^\infty=F_\infty v_t
}

The efficiency in Newtons per Watt is K=\langle F_y\rangle/\langle P\rangle=(2\pi/\lambda)(L/R)=v_t^{-1}. At v=v_t, Kv=1, corresponding to a lift-to-drag ratio of unity.

A.7 Halbach Peak Field

The peak surface field of a Halbach array is:

\boxed{
B_0=B_r[1-e^{-kd}]\frac{\sin(\pi/M)}{\pi/M}
}

where B_r is the magnet remanent field, d is the magnet thickness in the magnetization direction, and M is the number of magnet blocks per wavelength.

