Appendix B: Exponential Control Barrier Function Construction

B.1 Relative Degree and High-Order CBFs

For a constraint function h(x) with relative degree r\ge2, the control input does not appear in the first r-1 time derivatives. The r-th derivative takes the form:

h^{(r)}(x,u)=\mathcal{L}_f^{(r)}h(x)+\mathcal{L}_g\mathcal{L}_f^{(r-1)}h(x)u

The function h can be represented as the output of a linear system:

\eta(x,u)=\mathfrak{F}\eta(x)+\mathfrak{G}u,\qquad
h(x)=\mathbf{c}^T\eta(x)

with:

\eta(x)=\begin{bmatrix}
h(x)\\
\mathcal{L}_fh(x)\\
\vdots\\
\mathcal{L}_f^{(r-1)}h(x)
\end{bmatrix},\quad
\mathfrak{F}=\begin{bmatrix}
0&1&\cdots&0\\
\vdots&\vdots&&\vdots\\
0&0&\cdots&1\\
0&0&\cdots&0
\end{bmatrix},\quad
\mathfrak{G}=\begin{bmatrix}
0\\
\vdots\\
0\\
1
\end{bmatrix}

B.2 ECBF Definition

An r-times continuously differentiable function h:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R} is an exponential control barrier function (ECBF) for the system if there exists a vector \mathbf{k}_\alpha\in\mathbb{R}^r such that for all x\in\mathcal{C}:

\boxed{
h^{(r)}(x,u)+\sum_{i=0}^{r-1}k_i h^{(i)}(x)\ge0
}

The gains k_i are chosen such that the roots of the characteristic polynomial:

s^r+k_{r-1}s^{r-1}+\cdots+k_1s+k_0=0

have negative real parts.

B.3 Gap Confinement ECBF (Relative Degree 2)

For the lower gap constraint h=g-g_{\min}, the relative degree with respect to the active suspension force is 2:

g\xrightarrow{\text{integration}}\dot g\xrightarrow{\text{integration}}\ddot g\propto F_{\rm active}

The gap dynamics take the form:

\ddot g=f_g(x)+g_g(x)F_{\rm active}

The ECBF condition is:

\boxed{
L_f^2h+L_gL_fh\,F_{\rm active}+K_1L_fh+K_0h\ge0
}

where L_f^2h=f_g(x), L_gL_fh=g_g(x), and the gains are:

K_1=2\zeta\omega_n,\qquad K_0=\omega_n^2

With \omega_n=62.8 rad/s and \zeta=0.7:

K_1\approx88,\qquad K_0\approx3944

For the upper gap constraint \bar h=g_{\max}-g, the same structure applies with the sign of the gap dynamics reversed.

B.4 Wheel-Load Positivity CBF (Relative Degree 1)

For the wheel-load constraint:

h_W=F_{W,i}-F_{W,\min}

The relative degree with respect to the suspension force is 1:

\dot h_W+\alpha(h_W)\ge0

with \alpha(h_W)=\alpha_0 h_W, \alpha_0>0.

B.5 Power Dissipation CBF (Relative Degree 1)

For the power constraint:

h_D=P_{\max}-P_D(v,g)

The time derivative is:

\dot P_D=\frac{\partial P_D}{\partial v}\dot v+\frac{\partial P_D}{\partial g}\dot g

Both \dot v (controlled by propulsion) and \dot g (controlled by active suspension) appear. The control input is:

u=\begin{bmatrix}u_{\rm thrust}\\u_{\rm active}\end{bmatrix}

The CBF is relative degree 1 with respect to this combined input:

\dot h_D+\alpha(h_D)\ge0

B.6 Transition Rate CBF (Relative Degree 1)

From Lemma 1, with \lambda=F_M/(F_M+F_W):

\dot\lambda=\frac{F_W\dot F_M-F_M\dot F_W}{(F_M+F_W)^2}

and

\dot F_M=\frac{\partial F_M}{\partial v}\dot v
+\frac{\partial F_M}{\partial g}\dot g
+\dot F_{\rm active}

Define:

h_\lambda^+=\dot\lambda_{\max}-\dot\lambda\ge0,\qquad
h_\lambda^-=\dot\lambda_{\max}+\dot\lambda\ge0

Both are relative degree 1 with respect to \dot F_{\rm active}. Enforce via:

\dot h_\lambda^\pm+\alpha(h_\lambda^\pm)\ge0

