Appendix C: Capture Basin Computation

C.1 Viability Kernel

The viability kernel of the safe set \mathcal{K} is the set of initial states from which at least one control trajectory remains in \mathcal{K} forever:

\operatorname{Viab}(\mathcal{K})=\{x_0\in\mathcal{K}:\exists u(\cdot),\forall t\ge0,\;x(t)\in\mathcal{K}\}

C.2 Capture Basin Definition

The capture basin of the target \mathcal{C} viable in \mathcal{K} is the set of initial states from which at least one trajectory remains in \mathcal{K} until reaching \mathcal{C} in finite time:

\boxed{
\operatorname{Capt}(\mathcal{K},\mathcal{C})=\left\{
x_0\in\mathcal{K}:
\begin{aligned}
&\exists u(\cdot),\exists T\ge0,\\
&x(t)\in\mathcal{K}\;\forall t\in[0,T],\\
&x(T)\in\mathcal{C}
\end{aligned}
\right\}
}

C.3 Wheel-Supported Target Set

\mathcal{C}_{\rm wheel}=\left\{
x:
\begin{aligned}
&g_i\le g_{\rm contact}\\
&F_{W,i}\ge F_{W,\min}\\
&|\dot F_{W,i}|\le\dot F_{W,\max}\\
&|\ddot z|\le a_{z,\max}\\
&J_{\rm landing}\le J_{\rm absorbable}
\end{aligned}
\right\}

with the landing impact functional:

J_{\rm landing}=\frac12 m\dot z^2+\frac12 I_\theta\dot\theta^2+\frac12 I_\phi\dot\phi^2

C.4 Admissibility Condition

The fault-tolerant requirement is:

\boxed{
\forall f\in\mathcal{F}_{\rm admissible},\quad
x_0\in\mathcal{K}_{\rm operating}
\Rightarrow
x_0\in\operatorname{Capt}(\mathcal{K},\mathcal{C}_{\rm wheel})
}

Falsified if there exists x_0\in\mathcal{K}_{\rm operating} with x_0\notin\operatorname{Capt}(\mathcal{K},\mathcal{C}_{\rm wheel}).

C.5 CLF Sufficient Condition

If there exists a Lyapunov function V(x) and an invariant set \Omega\subset\mathcal{K} such that:

\dot V\le-\alpha V+\beta\|d\|

with \alpha,\beta>0, and \Omega\subseteq\operatorname{Capt}(\mathcal{K},\mathcal{C}_{\rm wheel}), then admissibility holds on \Omega.

C.6 Numerical Computation Strategy

For the reduced vertical–pitch model with 4 states (z,\dot z,\theta,\dot\theta), a grid-based computation on a 50^4 grid is feasible using the Saint-Pierre algorithm, which computes capture basins via viability theory for hybrid systems. For the full 6-DOF model with 12 states, sampling-based methods are required.

