Appendix D: Transition Rate Bound Derivation

D.1 True Time Derivative of \lambda

With:

\lambda=\frac{F_M}{F_M+F_W}

the time derivative is:

\dot\lambda=\frac{\dot F_M(F_M+F_W)-F_M(\dot F_M+\dot F_W)}{(F_M+F_W)^2}
=\frac{F_W\dot F_M-F_M\dot F_W}{(F_M+F_W)^2}

This is the correct expression, replacing the erroneous form \dot\lambda=\dot F_M/(mg).

D.2 Components of \dot F_M

\dot F_M=\frac{\partial F_M}{\partial v}\dot v
+\frac{\partial F_M}{\partial g}\dot g
+\dot F_{\rm active}

From Theorem 3:

\frac{\partial F_M}{\partial v}=F_M\frac{2v_t^2}{v(v^2+v_t^2)}

\frac{\partial F_M}{\partial g}=-2kF_M

D.3 Maximum Achievable Rate

The maximum achievable rate is:

\dot\lambda_{\max}^{\rm phys}
=
\max_{\rm admissible\;states}
\left|\frac{F_W\dot F_M-F_M\dot F_W}{(F_M+F_W)^2}\right|

This depends on:

· Actuator bandwidth \tau_F^{-1}
· Maximum active force F_{\rm active,max}^+
· Suspension stroke g_{\max}-g_{\min}
· Speed rate \dot v_{\max}
· Gap rate \dot g_{\max}
· Tyre vertical dynamics k_t

D.4 Design Value

For the reference vehicle, a conservative design value is:

\boxed{
\dot\lambda_{\max}^{\rm design}=0.5\;{\rm s}^{-1}
}

This is a design assumption, not a derived physical bound. Gate 4 of the numerical program (Section 11.2) computes the physically achievable value.

D.5 CBF Enforcement

h_\lambda^\pm=\dot\lambda_{\max}\pm\dot\lambda\ge0

\dot h_\lambda^\pm+\alpha(h_\lambda^\pm)\ge0

This prevents aggressive load dumps during mode transitions.

