Appendix E: Hybrid Corridor Graph

E.1 Graph Definition

\boxed{
G=(V,E_0\cup E_M)
}

where:

· V is the set of nodes (intersections, transition points, parking locations)
· E_0 is the set of ordinary asphalt edges where \lambda=0
· E_M is the set of magnetic-corridor edges where transition is permitted

E.2 Edge Attributes

Edge type Magnetic support Transition permitted Mode
E_0 No No 0 or 3
E_M Yes Yes if v\ge v_{\rm engage} 1, 2, or 3

E.3 Mode Transitions

· E_0\to E_M with v\ge v_{\rm engage}: Mode 0 → 1 → 2
· E_0\to E_M with v<v_{\rm engage}: Mode 0 remains, or Mode 1 partial
· E_M\to E_0: forced Mode 2 → 1 → 0 or Mode 2 → 3
· Fault on E_M: Mode 2 → 3 → \mathcal{C}_{\rm wheel}

E.4 Route Cost Functional

For a route P=(e_1,\dots,e_n):

\boxed{
J(P)=\int_P\left(P_{\rm prop}+P_D+\gamma T_{\rm transition}\right)dt
}

with \gamma>0 a penalty on transition time. The route planning problem is a hybrid optimal control problem on G, with the continuous dynamics of Sections 3–6 as the subsystem.

E.5 Engagement and Disengagement Constraints

The vehicle must choose engagement and disengagement points respecting:

v_{\rm engage}>v_{\rm phys},\qquad v_{\rm disengage}<v_{\rm phys}

For the gap-parameterized hierarchy:

v_{\rm disengage}<\inf_{g\in[g_{\min},g_{\max}]}v_{\rm phys}(g)

v_{\rm engage}>\sup_{g\in[g_{\min},g_{\max}]}v_{\rm rob}(g)

