Appendix H: Gap Sensitivity and Robustness Analysis

H.1 Logarithmic Sensitivities

From Theorem 3:

\frac{\partial\ln F_M}{\partial g}=-2k

\frac{\partial\ln F_M}{\partial v}
=\frac{2v_t^2}{v(v^2+v_t^2)}

H.2 Numerical Values

With k=31.4 m⁻¹:

S_g=-62.8\;{\rm m}^{-1}

An increase in gap of 1 mm multiplies the force by:

e^{-0.0628}\approx0.939

i.e., a \approx6.1\% decrease in lift per millimetre.

H.3 Speed Sensitivity

At v=8 m/s and v_t=1.6 m/s:

\frac{\partial\ln F_M}{\partial v}
=\frac{2(1.6)^2}{8(64+2.56)}
=\frac{5.12}{532.5}\approx0.0096\;{\rm s/m}

A 1 m/s speed increase produces a \approx0.96\% increase in lift.

H.4 Robustness Implication

The gap is the dominant sensitivity variable. The control system must prioritize gap regulation over speed regulation for maintaining lift authority.


